1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
How can a company report two billion dong in net profit at year-end, yet have only fifty million dong in the bank when a five hundred million payroll must be disbursed before New Year's Eve?

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,540
Under accrual accounting rules, delivering a ten billion contract books full revenue immediately.

3
00:00:16,540 --> 00:00:23,000
But the wholesale buyer pays only 7.5 billion upfront, leaving 2.5 billion uncollected under 60-day credit terms.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,610
Meanwhile, the company spent 7.45 billion on inventory and one billion on operating overhead.

5
00:00:28,610 --> 00:00:35,000
A massive 3.45 billion in liquid cash is sleeping trapped inside customer receivables and warehouse stock.

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:47,000
This is the classic overtrading growth trap: the company earned two billion in book profit, but Operating Cash Flow was negative 950 million dong because working capital swallowed up all available liquidity.

7
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:56,150
To avoid missing staff payroll on New Year's Eve, the profitable company was forced to borrow 450 million dong from the commercial bank at 10.5% interest.

8
00:00:56,150 --> 00:00:59,000
Profit on paper could not pay employee salaries.

9
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:06,410
Remember this vital corporate survival rule: accounting profit is merely an educated opinion, but cash flow is the oxygen of survival.

10
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:10,000
Never confuse book profit with spendable money in the bank vault.
